<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Madurai</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Madurai is an ancient city in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandya</a> kingdom. In the early fourteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-kafur">Malik Kafur</a>, general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alauddin-khalji">Alauddin Khalji</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>, sacked the Pandya treasury at Madurai. The campaign was part of the sultanate's most far-reaching expansion into southern India.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/madurai.jpg" alt="Madurai" width="320" height="157" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: KennyOMG · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Meenakshi_Amman_West_Tower.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…general Malik Kafur led the most spectacular campaigns in the sultanate's history, compelling the submission of the Kakatiyas at Warangal (1310), extracting enormous tribute from the Hoysala capital at Dwarasamudra and plundering the Pandya lands around <b>Madurai</b> (1311), bringing nearly every major southern Indian power into tributary submission.</blockquote><a href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>When Delhi’s grip loosened in the 1330s he took the offensive against the new Madurai Sultanate, but in 1343 he was captured near Tiruchirappalli and killed; Ibn Battuta, then at <b>Madurai</b>, reported that his flayed and stuffed body was displayed on the city wall.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hoysala-empire">Hoysala Empire · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>His successors expanded the kingdom across the southern plateau, absorbing neighbouring chiefs and resisting the Bijapur Sultanate, <b>Madurai</b> and other powers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mysore-1399-1799">Kingdom of Mysore (1399-1799) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Pandyas were one of the three ancient Tamil royal lineages, named with the Cholas and the Cheras in the Sangam poems and in Ashoka’s inscriptions, and associated with <b>Madurai</b> on the Vaigai river.</blockquote><a href="/empire/later-pandyan-empire">Later Pandyan Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Politically, the nayaka kingdoms that succeeded it, including <b>Madurai</b>, Thanjavur, Gingee, Ikkeri and the Wodeyars of Mysore, inherited its institutions and claimed its legitimacy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Hindu temple architecture produced some of the great monuments of Asia, from the temples of Khajuraho and the Meenakshi temple at <b>Madurai</b> to Angkor Wat and Prambanan.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
